# a-crew-on-one-front — plan 14 H2, and BOTH endings on one base.
#
# `a-base-on-two-fronts.keys` plus ONE line: `crew 7 0`.  The base has
# two fronts and the crew has one helper, so one front is covered and
# the other is not — and the two ends of the same wall ring tell the
# two stories plan 12 B7 asked to be distinguished, side by side, in
# one run.
#
#   nobody:                           77 ticks   ( 51 s)
#   this file — one helper east:     214 ticks   (143 s)
#   TWO helpers, both on the east:   214 ticks   — the second is worth
#                                                  NOTHING
#   one helper on each front:        242 ticks   (161 s)
#
# ⚠ **The east wall is BROKEN and the west wall is untouched at 97 of
# 100** — and it is the same wall, the same tower, the same waves.  On
# the covered front the helper ate the body ramp as fast as the tower
# built it, so the only way in was through; on the uncovered one a
# 1.0 m ramp stands and nobody ever had to hit the wall at all.
#
# ⚠ **A helper is worth 137 ticks here and its twin is worth nothing.**
# The two-helper-east run reads 214 exactly like this one: one tower
# makes about 0.03 m of body a tick and a vehicle clears 0.33, so the
# first helper on a front is already ten times faster than the front
# makes work.  What the second one is for is the OTHER front —
# `a-crew-on-two-fronts.keys` is that file, and it reads 242.
#
# ⚠ So the roster buys **coverage, not throughput**, and this pair is
# the measurement that separates them.  `tests/14_h2_the_roster.loft`
# § A second helper beside the first re-runs THIS file with its crew
# line doubled and gets every number in it back unchanged.

echo a crew on one front: one helper, one broken wall, and one ramp nobody ate

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
click 3 0
marker 3 0 tower
click -3 0
marker -3 0 tower

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click -11 0
marker -11 0 spawn 0

count markers 5 5
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -12 -2 12 -2
drag -12 -1 12 -1
drag -12 0 12 0
drag -12 1 12 1
drag -12 2 12 2
count painted 125 125

palette 9
click 5 -2
click 5 -1
click 5 0
click 5 1
click 5 2
click 5 -4
click 5 -3
click 5 3
click 5 4
click -5 -2
click -5 -1
click -5 0
click -5 1
click -5 2
click -5 -4
click -5 -3
click -5 3
click -5 4
kind 5 0 wall
kind -5 0 wall
hp 5 0 100.0 100.0
hp -5 0 100.0 100.0

# ⚠ **The whole difference from `a-base-on-two-fronts.keys`.**  ONE
# helper, parked one hex clear of where the east tower's kills will
# fall.  No key is pressed and none exists: clearing is a POSITION
# (`DESIGN.md` § 11), so what the player decides is where the crew
# STANDS — and this file is the decision to cover one side.
crew 7 0
helper 0 7 0

wave 10
count alive 10 10
wallet 200.0 200.0

tick 22
wave 16

fall 800
ticks 130 140
wallet 0.0 0.0

# ── ⚠ The two endings, one base ─────────────────────────────────

# EAST — covered.  No ramp: what the tower does leave at the wall, the
# helper eats as fast as it appears.
#
# ⚠ **The east wall BREAKS again since plan 24 W1**, where plan 16 W2's
# pre-walk window had made it survive the run.  The front is the wall
# FACE's width now rather than the approach fan's, so the east front
# gets enough robots onto the wall to chew through it before the base
# falls from the west.  ⚠ Nothing about the crew moved — the pile is
# still zero, so the helper is still eating the ramp as fast as the
# tower makes it.
pile 6 0 0.0 0.0
kind 5 0 grass

# WEST — uncovered.  A 1.0 m ramp stands where the bodies fell, and the
# wall behind it is untouched: they walked over their own dead and never
# touched it.  ⚠ That is plan 12 B7's ending, still happening, on the
# front nobody was standing on — and W2 moved it one row north, because
# the window changed where the kills land.
pile -6 -1 0.9 1.1
kind -5 -1 wall
hp -5 -1 95.0 100.0

# The helper stayed where it was put — `plans/14` § Open questions 1.
helper 0 7 0

snap h2_a_crew_on_one_front
